//? Title: Sessions Without Sessions: Feeds, Fetches, Targets and a Per-Op Profiler
//? Subtitle: What a TensorFlow session did, what it becomes in an eager library, what one call costs, and where the time of a training step goes, using datamunge's nn::Profiler
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A TensorFlow 1 session runs part of a graph on request. Each call names the tensors to feed and fetch and the operations to run for their side effects,
and can return a per-op trace. In an eager library such as `datamunge::nn` every operation runs when it is called. Feeds and fetches become function
arguments and return values, targets become function calls made for their side effects, and a pre-built callable becomes a `std::function`. This report shows that mapping on the
original example's model, with its outputs derived in closed form. It then measures what a call costs. A three-op call takes
\(T(n)=\alpha+\beta n\) with a fixed cost \(\alpha\) of 0.88 µs and 3.8 ns per batch row, fitted to within 7%. Below about 230 rows the fixed cost dominates, and a
`std::function` adds nothing measurable. The missing piece was RunMetadata's per-op trace. The library gained `nn::Profiler`, `nn::ProfileScope` and
`nn::plot_timeline`. They time user-named regions and every node the autograd engine executes, at about 55 ns per event, and export a Chrome trace.
On a ten-predictor regression for `mtcars`, Adam reaches the normal-equations weights to \(2.6\times10^{-9}\). The profile shows that the five forward
ops and five backward nodes of a step each cost a fraction of a microsecond. The autograd engine's own bookkeeping takes about as long as the backward nodes it runs.
)

\toc

> The problem

TensorFlow 1 separates building a graph from running it (Abadi et al., 2016). A `Session` owns a copy of the graph, and `Session::Run` takes three lists:
*feeds*, values for chosen tensors (usually placeholders); *fetches*, the tensors whose values to return; and *targets*, operations to run for
their side effects, such as a variable update. `RunOptions` can request a full trace, returned in `RunMetadata` as per-op start times and durations, and
`MakeCallable` fixes a feed, fetch and target signature once so that repeated calls skip the set-up.

`datamunge::nn` is eager, like TensorFlow 2 and PyTorch (Agrawal et al., 2019; Paszke et al., 2019). There is no graph to run, only C++ code, so most of
the session's machinery disappears. Two questions remain. What does a call cost, now that each op is dispatched one at a time? And how do we see
where the time goes, now that there is no `RunMetadata`?

This report replaces the original example `examples/nn/0_foundations/0.1_tensors_graphs_sessions/sessions.cpp`, which printed hand-made stage timings because
the library could not trace itself. That gap is filled by `nn::Profiler`.

> The method

>> A session as a function

Write the example's graph as a composition. The model computes \(xw = xW\), then \(y = xw + b\), then \(s = \sum_{ij} y_{ij}\), so
\(s = h(g(f(x)))\) with \(f(x)=xW\), \(g(v)=v+b\) and \(h(y)=\sum y\). A session call with feed \(x\) and fetch \(s\) evaluates the whole composition. Feeding the
intermediate \(xw\) evaluates only the suffix \(h\circ g\), skipping \(f\). Eagerly, both are ordinary calls: `y_sum(y(x))` and `y_sum(y_from_xw(v))`.
A missing feed is a missing argument. It cannot occur if the argument is required, and an undefined `Tensor` stands in for an unfed placeholder, which the model rejects
with an exception. A target is a function called for its side effect, here `step.add_(1)` on a counter tensor. A callable is a closure that fixes the
feed, fetch and target together.

>> The cost of a call

An eager call dispatches each op separately: shape checks, output allocation and, when gradients are recorded, a tape node. That fixed cost does not depend on the size of the
data, while the arithmetic grows with it. For a batch of \(n\) rows, model the time per call as

$$
T(n)=\alpha+\beta n .
$$
\alttext(T of n equals alpha plus beta n.)

Timings are noisier for small calls, so fit by weighted least squares with weights \(w_i=1/T_i^2\), which minimises the sum of squared *relative* errors
\(\sum_i\bigl((T_i-\alpha-\beta n_i)/T_i\bigr)^2\). Setting the derivatives to zero gives the normal equations

$$
\begin{pmatrix}\sum w_i&\sum w_in_i\\\sum w_in_i&\sum w_in_i^2\end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix}\alpha\\\beta\end{pmatrix}=\begin{pmatrix}\sum w_iT_i\\\sum w_in_iT_i\end{pmatrix}.
$$
\alttext(A two by two linear system. The matrix has entries sum of w i, sum of w i n i, sum of w i n i, and sum of w i n i squared. It multiplies the vector alpha, beta, and equals the vector with entries sum of w i T i and sum of w i n i T i.)

The fixed cost's share of a call is \(\alpha/(\alpha+\beta n)\). It is one half at the batch size \(n^\ast=\alpha/\beta\), and the time per row \(T(n)/n=\beta+\alpha/n\) falls towards
\(\beta\) as the fixed cost is spread over more rows. Each time \(T_i\) is the fastest of seven blocks of repeated calls. Interference from the rest of the machine only ever
adds time, so the minimum is the most reproducible estimate (Chen and Revels, 2016).

>> Tracing

A trace is a list of events, each with a name, a start time \(s_e\), a duration \(d_e\) and a nesting depth on its thread. `nn::ProfileScope` records one event for its
own lifetime while an `nn::Profiler` is active. The autograd engine wraps each node it executes in a scope of category "backward", so a gradient
computation is traced op by op without any change to user code. Scopes nest. The *self time* of an event is its duration minus the durations of the events directly inside it:

$$
\operatorname{self}(e)=d_e-\sum_{c\,:\,\operatorname{parent}(c)=e}d_c .
$$
\alttext(The self time of event e is its duration d e minus the sum of the durations d c of the events c whose parent is e.)

The self times of an outermost event and everything nested in it add up to its duration exactly, so a table of self times divides the traced wall time
without counting anything twice. When no profiler is active a scope does one atomic load and records nothing. `Profiler::to_chrome_trace()` writes the events in the
trace-event format read by `chrome://tracing` and Perfetto.

> Simple examples

>> The session concepts on the original model

The original example's model has \(W=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&1\\1&1\end{pmatrix}\) and \(b=(0.5,-0.5)\), and row \(r\) of a batch is \((r,r+1,r+2)\).

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

// The "graph" of the original example: y = x W + b, its sum, and a step counter.
struct Model {
  Tensor w    = nn::tensor({{1, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 1}});
  Tensor b    = nn::tensor({0.5, -0.5});
  Tensor step = nn::scalar(0, nn::DType::Int64);
  // An undefined or wrongly shaped x plays the part of an unfed placeholder.
  Tensor xw(const Tensor& x) const {
    if (!x.defined()) throw std::invalid_argument("x: no value supplied");
    if (x.dim() != 2 || x.shape()[1] != 3) throw std::invalid_argument("x: expected shape [?, 3], got " + nn::shape_str(x.shape()));
    return nn::matmul(x, w);
  }
  Tensor y_from_xw(const Tensor& v) const { return nn::add(v, b); }
  Tensor y(const Tensor& x) const { return y_from_xw(xw(x)); }
  static Tensor y_sum(const Tensor& y) { return nn::sum(y); }
  void step_inc() { step.add_(nn::scalar(1, nn::DType::Int64)); }
};

// Row r of the batch is (r, r + 1, r + 2).
Tensor batch(int n) {
  std::vector<float> v;
  for (int r = 0; r < n; ++r) for (int c = 0; c < 3; ++c) v.push_back(float(r + c));
  return nn::from_vector(v, {n, 3});
}

int main() {
  Model m;
  std::print("| session concept | eager form | result |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n");
  std::print("| fetch y, feed x (batch 2) | `m.y(batch(2))` | {} |\n", m.y(batch(2)).values_str(8));
  std::print("| fetch y_sum (batch 2) | `Model::y_sum(m.y(batch(2)))` | {} |\n", Model::y_sum(m.y(batch(2))).item<float>());
  std::print("| feed the intermediate xw = [[100, 200]] | `m.y_from_xw(...)` | {} |\n", m.y_from_xw(nn::tensor({{100, 200}})).values_str(8));
  for (const Tensor& bad : {Tensor(), nn::zeros({2, 4})}) {
    try { (void)m.y(bad); } catch (const std::invalid_argument& e) { std::print("| missing or malformed feed | `m.y({})` | throws: {} |\n", bad.defined() ? "zeros({2, 4})" : "Tensor()", e.what()); }
  }
  for (int i = 0; i < 3; ++i) m.step_inc();
  std::print("| target only, three times | `m.step_inc()` | step = {} |\n", m.step.item<std::int64_t>());
  const std::function<Tensor(const Tensor&)> callable = [&m](const Tensor& x) { Tensor s = Model::y_sum(m.y(x)); m.step_inc(); return s; };
  const float cs = callable(batch(2)).item<float>();
  std::print("| callable: feed x, fetch y_sum, target step | `callable(batch(2))` | y_sum = {}, step = {} |\n\n", cs, m.step.item<std::int64_t>());

  // Closed form: row r gives y = (2r + 2.5, 2r + 2.5), so y_sum(n) = 2n^2 + 3n.
  int worst = 0;
  double max_err = 0;
  for (int n = 1; n <= 1024; ++n) {
    const double got = Model::y_sum(m.y(batch(n))).item<float>(), want = 2.0 * n * n + 3.0 * n;
    if (std::abs(got - want) > max_err) max_err = std::abs(got - want), worst = n;
  }
  std::print("Closed form 2n² + 3n against y_sum for n = 1, ..., 1024: largest difference {}; n = 64 gives {}.\n", max_err,
             Model::y_sum(m.y(batch(64))).item<float>());
  (void)worst;
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| session concept | eager form | result |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| fetch y, feed x (batch 2) | `m.y(batch(2))` | [[2.5, 2.5], [4.5, 4.5]] |
| fetch y_sum (batch 2) | `Model::y_sum(m.y(batch(2)))` | 14 |
| feed the intermediate xw = [[100, 200]] | `m.y_from_xw(...)` | [[100.5, 199.5]] |
| missing or malformed feed | `m.y(Tensor())` | throws: x: no value supplied |
| missing or malformed feed | `m.y(zeros({2, 4}))` | throws: x: expected shape [?, 3], got [2, 4] |
| target only, three times | `m.step_inc()` | step = 3 |
| callable: feed x, fetch y_sum, target step | `callable(batch(2))` | y_sum = 14, step = 4 |

Closed form 2n² + 3n against y_sum for n = 1, ..., 1024: largest difference 0; n = 64 gives 8384.
// result_end

Every entry follows from the composition by hand. Row \(r\) gives \(xw=(r+(r+2),\,(r+1)+(r+2))=(2r+2,\,2r+3)\) and \(y=(2r+2.5,\,2r+2.5)\), so for \(n=2\) the rows are
\((2.5,2.5)\) and \((4.5,4.5)\). Summing over the rows,

$$
s(n)=\sum_{r=0}^{n-1}2(2r+2.5)=2n(n-1)+5n=2n^2+3n ,
$$
\alttext(s of n equals the sum for r from 0 to n minus 1 of 2 times 2 r plus 2.5, which is 2 n times n minus 1 plus 5 n, which is 2 n squared plus 3 n.)

which gives 14 for \(n=2\) and 8384 for \(n=64\), the values the original example checked. The program confirms the closed form for every \(n\) up to 1024 with no
difference at all. Every value is a multiple of one half and far below \(2^{23}\), so float32 represents each one exactly. Feeding the intermediate \(xw=(100,200)\) skips the product and returns
\((100.5,199.5)\). The two bad feeds are rejected before any arithmetic, and the counter shows that three target-only calls and one callable call each ran the update once.

> Checking the theory

>> The fixed cost of a call

The next program times `y_sum(y(x))`, three ops with no gradient recording, for batches of \(n=1\) to 16 384 rows. It times the same work called directly,
through a `std::function`, and inside a `ProfileScope` while a profiler records. It then fits \(T(n)=\alpha+\beta n\) to each.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sessions_overhead.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;
using clk = std::chrono::steady_clock;

struct Model {
  Tensor w = nn::tensor({{1, 0}, {0, 1}, {1, 1}});
  Tensor b = nn::tensor({0.5, -0.5});
  Tensor y(const Tensor& x) const { return nn::add(nn::matmul(x, w), b); }
};

// Fastest of 7 blocks of the time per call, in microseconds; each block runs about 20 ms.
template <class F> double time_call(F&& f) {
  int reps = 1;
  for (;;) {
    const auto t0 = clk::now();
    for (int i = 0; i < reps; ++i) f();
    if (std::chrono::duration<double>(clk::now() - t0).count() > 0.02) break;
    reps *= 2;
  }
  std::vector<double> blocks;
  for (int b = 0; b < 7; ++b) {
    const auto t0 = clk::now();
    for (int i = 0; i < reps; ++i) f();
    blocks.push_back(std::chrono::duration<double, std::micro>(clk::now() - t0).count() / reps);
  }
  std::ranges::sort(blocks);
  return blocks[0];
}

// Weighted least squares T = alpha + beta n with weights 1 / T^2 (relative errors).
std::pair<double, double> fit(const std::vector<double>& n, const std::vector<double>& t) {
  double s0 = 0, s1 = 0, s2 = 0, r0 = 0, r1 = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < n.size(); ++i) {
    const double w = 1 / (t[i] * t[i]);
    s0 += w, s1 += w * n[i], s2 += w * n[i] * n[i], r0 += w * t[i], r1 += w * n[i] * t[i];
  }
  const double det = s0 * s2 - s1 * s1;
  return {(s2 * r0 - s1 * r1) / det, (s0 * r1 - s1 * r0) / det};
}

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  nn::NoGradGuard no_grad;
  Model m;
  const std::function<Tensor(const Tensor&)> callable = [&m](const Tensor& x) { return nn::sum(m.y(x)); };
  nn::Profiler prof;
  std::vector<double> ns, td, tf, tp;
  volatile float sink = 0;
  nn::Generator gen(7);
  for (int n = 1; n <= 16384; n *= 2) {
    const Tensor x = nn::normal({n, 3}, 0, 1, gen);
    ns.push_back(n);
    td.push_back(time_call([&] { sink = sink + nn::sum(m.y(x)).item<float>(); }));
    tf.push_back(time_call([&] { sink = sink + callable(x).item<float>(); }));
    prof.start();
    tp.push_back(time_call([&] { nn::ProfileScope s("step"); sink = sink + nn::sum(m.y(x)).item<float>(); }));
    prof.stop();
  }
  const auto [ad, bd] = fit(ns, td);
  const auto [af, bf] = fit(ns, tf);
  const auto [ap, bp] = fit(ns, tp);
  std::print("| call | α (fixed cost per call) | β (cost per row) | half-overhead batch α/β |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  std::print("| direct | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} ns | {:.0f} |\n", ad, 1e3 * bd, ad / bd);
  std::print("| std::function | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} ns | {:.0f} |\n", af, 1e3 * bf, af / bf);
  std::print("| direct, inside a ProfileScope (profiler on) | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} ns | {:.0f} |\n\n", ap, 1e3 * bp, ap / bp);
  std::print("| batch n | direct | std::function | profiled | fit α + βn | fixed-cost share α / T |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  double worst_fit = 0;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < ns.size(); ++i) {
    const double pred = ad + bd * ns[i];
    worst_fit = std::max(worst_fit, std::abs(td[i] / pred - 1));
    std::print("| {} | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} µs | {:.2f} µs | {:.0f}% |\n", ns[i], td[i], tf[i], tp[i], pred, 100 * ad / td[i]);
  }
  std::print("\nLargest relative misfit of the direct-call line: {:.0f}%.\n\n", 100 * worst_fit);

  // The cost of a scope by itself: the fastest of 7 blocks of 200 000 empty scopes, with the
  // recorded events cleared between blocks.
  auto scope_ns = [&](bool on) {
    double best = 1e300;
    if (on) prof.start();
    for (int block = 0; block < 7; ++block) {
      prof.clear();
      const auto t0 = clk::now();
      for (int i = 0; i < 200000; ++i) nn::ProfileScope s("empty");
      best = std::min(best, std::chrono::duration<double, std::nano>(clk::now() - t0).count() / 200000);
    }
    if (on) prof.stop();
    return best;
  };
  const double off = scope_ns(false), on = scope_ns(true);
  std::print("An empty ProfileScope costs {:.1f} ns with no profiler active and {:.1f} ns while recording ({} events in the last block).\n",
             off, on, prof.events().size());

  // Left: time per call, log-log. Right: time per row, which falls to beta once alpha is amortised.
  auto lg = [](std::vector<double> v) { for (auto& e : v) e = std::log10(e); return v; };
  std::vector<double> ln = lg(ns), fitted, per_row_d, per_row_f, fit_row;
  for (std::size_t i = 0; i < ns.size(); ++i) {
    fitted.push_back(std::log10(ad + bd * ns[i]));
    per_row_d.push_back(std::log10(1e3 * td[i] / ns[i]));
    per_row_f.push_back(std::log10(1e3 * tf[i] / ns[i]));
    fit_row.push_back(std::log10(1e3 * (ad / ns[i] + bd)));
  }
  const dm::graphics::RGB blue{37, 99, 235}, orange{234, 88, 12}, grey{148, 163, 184}, green{22, 163, 74};
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.lines(ln, fitted, "fit α + βn (direct)", grey, 4.0);
  left.lines(ln, lg(td), "direct", blue, 2.0).points(ln, lg(td), "", blue, 5.0);
  left.lines(ln, lg(tf), "std::function", orange, 1.5).points(ln, lg(tf), "", orange, 4.0);
  left.lines(ln, lg(tp), "profiled", green, 1.5).points(ln, lg(tp), "", green, 4.0);
  const double ymax = std::ceil(std::log10(std::max({td.back(), tf.back(), tp.back()})) * 2) / 2;
  left.x_limits(0, std::log10(16384.0)).y_limits(-0.5, ymax + 1).title("Time per call").x_label("log10 batch rows n").y_label("log10 µs");
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  right.lines(ln, fit_row, "fit α/n + β", grey, 4.0);
  right.lines(ln, per_row_d, "direct", blue, 2.0).points(ln, per_row_d, "", blue, 5.0);
  right.lines(ln, per_row_f, "std::function", orange, 1.5).points(ln, per_row_f, "", orange, 4.0);
  const double rmin = std::floor(std::log10(1e3 * bd) * 2) / 2 - 0.5;
  right.x_limits(0, std::log10(16384.0)).y_limits(rmin, std::ceil(per_row_d.front() * 2) / 2 + 0.5)
      .title("Time per row").x_label("log10 batch rows n").y_label("log10 ns per row");
  auto plot = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  plot.add(left);
  plot.add(right);
  plot.size(1100, 440).fit_panels();
  plot.save("build/sessions_overhead.svg");
  (void)rmin;
}
```
