\image(figures/sessions_overhead.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Time per call and time per row against batch size, on log scales, for a direct call (blue), the same call through a std::function (orange) and inside an active ProfileScope (green). Grey: the fitted line \(\alpha+\beta n\) for the direct call, and \(\alpha/n+\beta\) on the right.)
\alttext(Left: log time per call against log batch size from 0 to 4.2. All three curves lie almost on top of each other, flat just below 0, that is just under 1 microsecond, up to about log n equals 1.5, then rising with slope one to about 1.8, which is 63 microseconds, at 16384 rows. The green profiled curve sits slightly above the others at small n. Right: log nanoseconds per row falls along a straight line of slope minus one from 2.9 at one row and levels off near 0.6, about 4 nanoseconds, from log n equals 3 on, matching the grey fitted curve.)

The line fits every batch size to within 7%, with a fixed cost of \(\alpha=0.88\) µs and \(\beta=3.8\) ns per row. The largest misfit is at
\(n=256\) and 512, where the measured times bend slightly above the line. The two costs are equal at \(n^\ast=\alpha/\beta\approx230\) rows. Below about 16 rows a call costs
nearly the same whatever its size, and at \(n=1\) the arithmetic is lost in the dispatch. The fixed-cost share \(\alpha/T\) reads 101% there only because the fit's \(\alpha\) is a hair above the measured time.
The call does three ops (`matmul`, `add`, `sum`), so each op costs roughly 0.3 µs before it touches the data.

Two of TensorFlow's motivations for callables do not carry over. Calling through a `std::function` costs the same as calling directly, to the
reported precision, at every batch size, because one indirect call is a few nanoseconds against 880. Profiling is cheap too. An idle `ProfileScope` costs 2.8 ns. A
recording one costs 55 ns, close to the 0.08 µs that wrapping the call in a scope adds to \(\alpha\) and leaves \(\beta\) unchanged. The engine's per-node
events cost the same, so tracing a step of fifteen events adds under a microsecond.

> A dataset: tracing a training loop on mtcars

The `mtcars` data (Henderson and Velleman, 1981) give fuel consumption and ten design variables for 32 cars. Regress centred `mpg` on all ten standardised variables with a linear
model \(\hat y=Xw\) and mean squared error. The minimiser solves the normal equations \(X^\top Xw=X^\top y\), which `nn::solve` gives directly. Training
should reach the same \(w\). The training step is written as the session example's callable. It feeds \(X\) and \(y\), fetches the loss, and has the
Adam update and the step counter as targets (Kingma and Ba, 2015). Each stage sits in a named `ProfileScope`.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sessions_training.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F64  = nn::DType::Float64;
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"cyl", "disp", "hp", "drat", "wt", "qsec", "vs", "am", "gear", "carb"};
  const std::int64_t n = 32, p = static_cast<std::int64_t>(names.size());
  // Standardised predictors and centred mpg, so the intercept is the mean and drops out.
  std::vector<double> xv(n * p), yv = cars.get_column<double>("mpg").values();
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < p; ++j) {
    const auto c = cars.get_column<double>(names[j]).values();
    const double mu = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n;
    double ss = 0;
    for (double v : c) ss += (v - mu) * (v - mu);
    const double sd = std::sqrt(ss / (n - 1));
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) xv[i * p + j] = (c[i] - mu) / sd;
  }
  const double ybar = std::accumulate(yv.begin(), yv.end(), 0.0) / n;
  for (auto& v : yv) v -= ybar;
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(xv, {n, p}), y = nn::from_vector(yv, {n, 1});

  // Least squares by the normal equations: the answer training should reach.
  const Tensor beta = nn::solve(nn::matmul(nn::transpose(X), X), nn::matmul(nn::transpose(X), y));
  const double ols_loss = nn::mean(nn::square(nn::matmul(X, beta) - y)).item();

  // The "callable": feeds X and y, fetches the loss, targets the update and the step counter.
  nn::ParameterStore store(3, F64);
  const Tensor w = store.get("linear/w", {p, 1}, nn::init::zeros());
  nn::Optimizer opt(store, 0.05, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
  Tensor step = nn::scalar(0, nn::DType::Int64);
  const std::function<double(const Tensor&, const Tensor&)> train_step = [&](const Tensor& x, const Tensor& t) {
    nn::ProfileScope s("step");
    Tensor pred, loss;
    {
      nn::ProfileScope f("forward");
      pred = nn::matmul(x, w);
    }
    {
      nn::ProfileScope l("loss");
      loss = nn::mean(nn::square(pred - t));
    }
    std::vector<Tensor> g;
    {
      nn::ProfileScope b("grad");
      g = nn::grad({loss}, {w});
    }
    {
      nn::ProfileScope u("update");
      opt.step(g);
      step.add_(nn::scalar(1, nn::DType::Int64));
    }
    return loss.item();
  };
  // One untraced step to count the recorded forward ops.
  const std::size_t forward_ops = nn::tape({nn::mean(nn::square(nn::matmul(X, w) - y))}).op_count();

  const int steps = 3000;
  std::vector<double> gap;
  nn::Profiler prof;
  prof.start();
  for (int k = 0; k < steps; ++k) gap.push_back(train_step(X, y) - ols_loss);
  prof.stop();

  int below = -1;
  for (int k = 0; k < steps; ++k) if (below < 0 && gap[k] < 1e-12) below = k + 1;
  std::print("The loss gap first falls below 1e-12 at step {}; over the last 100 steps it lies between {:.1e} and {:.1e}.\n",
             below, *std::min_element(gap.end() - 100, gap.end()), *std::max_element(gap.end() - 100, gap.end()));
  const Tensor diff = w.detach() - beta;
  std::print("After {} steps (step counter {}): training loss {:.6f}, least-squares loss {:.6f}, largest |w − β| = {:.1e}.\n\n",
             steps, step.item<std::int64_t>(), gap.back() + ols_loss, ols_loss, nn::max(nn::abs(diff)).item());
  std::print("| predictor | trained w | normal equations β |\n| :--- | ---: | ---: |\n");
  const auto wv = w.to_vector<double>(), bv = beta.to_vector<double>();
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < p; ++j) std::print("| {} | {:.4f} | {:.4f} |\n", names[j], wv[j], bv[j]);
  const auto ev = prof.events();
  std::vector<double> step_start, step_us;
  std::size_t backward_events = 0;
  for (const auto& e : ev) {
    if (e.name == "step") step_start.push_back(e.start_us), step_us.push_back(e.duration_us);
    if (e.category == "backward") ++backward_events;
  }
  std::ranges::sort(step_start);
  std::vector<double> sorted = step_us;
  std::ranges::sort(sorted);
  std::print("\nStep time: median {:.1f} µs, 10th to 90th percentile {:.1f} to {:.1f} µs, longest {:.0f} µs.\n",
             sorted[steps / 2], sorted[steps / 10], sorted[9 * steps / 10], sorted.back());
  (void)forward_ops, (void)backward_events;

  // Convergence and the distribution of step times.
  std::vector<double> ks, lg;
  for (int k = 0; k < steps; ++k) ks.push_back(k + 1), lg.push_back(std::clamp(std::log10(std::max(gap[k], 1e-16)), -16.0, 2.0));
  auto left = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  left.lines(ks, lg, "", dm::graphics::RGB{37, 99, 235}, 2.0);
  left.x_limits(0, steps).y_limits(-16, 2).title("Loss above least squares").x_label("step").y_label("log10 (loss − least-squares loss)");
  // Step times up to the 99th percentile; the slowest 1% are counted in the title.
  const double cut = sorted[99 * steps / 100];
  std::vector<double> kept;
  for (double t : step_us) if (t <= cut) kept.push_back(t);
  auto right = dm::graphics::RPlot::hist(kept, 40, "", dm::graphics::RGB{96, 165, 250});
  right.title(std::format("Step times ({} slowest not shown)", steps - kept.size())).x_label("µs per step").y_label("steps");
  auto fig = dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1, 2);
  fig.add(left);
  fig.add(right);
  fig.size(1100, 440).fit_panels();
  fig.save("build/sessions_training.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The loss gap first falls below 1e-12 at step 2451; over the last 100 steps it lies between -1.8e-15 and 8.9e-16.
After 3000 steps (step counter 3000): training loss 4.609201, least-squares loss 4.609201, largest |w − β| = 2.6e-09.

| predictor | trained w | normal equations β |
| :--- | ---: | ---: |
| cyl | -0.1990 | -0.1990 |
| disp | 1.6528 | 1.6528 |
| hp | -1.4729 | -1.4729 |
| drat | 0.4209 | 0.4209 |
| wt | -3.6353 | -3.6353 |
| qsec | 1.4672 | 1.4672 |
| vs | 0.1602 | 0.1602 |
| am | 1.2576 | 1.2576 |
| gear | 0.4836 | 0.4836 |
| carb | -0.3221 | -0.3221 |

Step time: median 12.6 µs, 10th to 90th percentile 9.7 to 15.8 µs, longest 832 µs.
\image(figures/sessions_training.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Left: the training loss minus the least-squares loss, on a log scale, over 3000 Adam steps; values at or below \(10^{-16}\), the float64 rounding level, are drawn at \(-16\). Right: the distribution of the time per training step, without the slowest 1%.)
\alttext(Left: the log loss gap starts near 2, drops to about minus 0.4 within the first 100 steps, then falls along a curve that steepens, passing minus 5 near step 1500 and minus 12 near step 2450, and reaches the floor of minus 16 at about step 2700, after which it flickers along the floor. Right: a histogram of step times from 5.1 to 7.1 microseconds with a tall peak at about 5.2 and a second, smaller peak near 5.6.)

Training reaches the least-squares solution. The loss gap falls below \(10^{-12}\) at step 2451. In the last 100 steps it lies between
\(-1.8\times10^{-15}\) and \(8.9\times10^{-16}\), a few units of float64 rounding in a loss of 4.6, so the trained loss is exactly the least-squares loss to working
precision. Every trained weight agrees with the normal-equations solution to \(2.6\times10^{-9}\). The step counter, the example's target, reads 3000.
Convergence is slow at first and then accelerates. The predictors are strongly correlated (`disp`, `hp`, `wt` and `cyl` all measure size), which makes the loss a long, narrow valley,
and Adam crosses such a valley quickly but moves along it slowly until its step sizes adapt.

A step takes about 5.2 µs, and nine in ten steps take between 5.2 and 5.6 µs. The histogram has a second, smaller mode near 5.6 µs, and 1% of steps take much longer,
up to a few hundred microseconds, outliers of the kind any shared machine produces. A model this small is all fixed cost: the 32 by 10 product is a few hundred
flops, a few nanoseconds of arithmetic against a dispatch cost of hundreds.

The same loop, profiled again, gives the table of where the time goes and a timeline of three consecutive steps.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/sessions_timeline.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

namespace nn = dm::nn;
using nn::Tensor;

int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto F64  = nn::DType::Float64;
  const auto cars = dm::datasets::mtcars();
  const std::vector<std::string> names{"cyl", "disp", "hp", "drat", "wt", "qsec", "vs", "am", "gear", "carb"};
  const std::int64_t n = 32, p = static_cast<std::int64_t>(names.size());
  // Standardised predictors and centred mpg, so the intercept is the mean and drops out.
  std::vector<double> xv(n * p), yv = cars.get_column<double>("mpg").values();
  for (std::int64_t j = 0; j < p; ++j) {
    const auto c = cars.get_column<double>(names[j]).values();
    const double mu = std::accumulate(c.begin(), c.end(), 0.0) / n;
    double ss = 0;
    for (double v : c) ss += (v - mu) * (v - mu);
    const double sd = std::sqrt(ss / (n - 1));
    for (std::int64_t i = 0; i < n; ++i) xv[i * p + j] = (c[i] - mu) / sd;
  }
  const double ybar = std::accumulate(yv.begin(), yv.end(), 0.0) / n;
  for (auto& v : yv) v -= ybar;
  const Tensor X = nn::from_vector(xv, {n, p}), y = nn::from_vector(yv, {n, 1});

  // The "callable": feeds X and y, fetches the loss, targets the update and the step counter.
  nn::ParameterStore store(3, F64);
  const Tensor w = store.get("linear/w", {p, 1}, nn::init::zeros());
  nn::Optimizer opt(store, 0.05, {.kind = nn::OptimizerKind::Adam});
  Tensor step = nn::scalar(0, nn::DType::Int64);
  const std::function<double(const Tensor&, const Tensor&)> train_step = [&](const Tensor& x, const Tensor& t) {
    nn::ProfileScope s("step");
    Tensor pred, loss;
    {
      nn::ProfileScope f("forward");
      pred = nn::matmul(x, w);
    }
    {
      nn::ProfileScope l("loss");
      loss = nn::mean(nn::square(pred - t));
    }
    std::vector<Tensor> g;
    {
      nn::ProfileScope b("grad");
      g = nn::grad({loss}, {w});
    }
    {
      nn::ProfileScope u("update");
      opt.step(g);
      step.add_(nn::scalar(1, nn::DType::Int64));
    }
    return loss.item();
  };
  // One untraced step to count the recorded forward ops.
  const std::size_t forward_ops = nn::tape({nn::mean(nn::square(nn::matmul(X, w) - y))}).op_count();

  const int steps = 3000;
  nn::Profiler prof;
  prof.start();
  for (int k = 0; k < steps; ++k) (void)train_step(X, y);
  prof.stop();
  std::print("Profile of all {} steps (wall time {:.1f} ms):\n\n{}\n", steps, prof.wall_us() / 1e3, prof.to_markdown(12));

  const auto ev = prof.events();
  std::vector<double> step_start, step_us;
  std::size_t backward_events = 0;
  for (const auto& e : ev) {
    if (e.name == "step") step_start.push_back(e.start_us), step_us.push_back(e.duration_us);
    if (e.category == "backward") ++backward_events;
  }
  std::ranges::sort(step_start);
  std::vector<double> sorted = step_us;
  std::ranges::sort(sorted);
  std::print("Per step: {} forward ops on the tape, {:.1f} backward nodes executed; step time median {:.1f} µs, "
             "10th to 90th percentile {:.1f} to {:.1f} µs, longest {:.0f} µs.\n",
             forward_ops, double(backward_events) / steps, sorted[steps / 2], sorted[steps / 10], sorted[9 * steps / 10], sorted.back());

  // The timeline of three steps in the middle of training.
  nn::plot_timeline(prof, "Steps 1000 to 1002 of the mtcars regression", step_start[1000], step_start[1003])
      .save("build/sessions_timeline.svg");

}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Profile of all 3000 steps (wall time 57.7 ms):

| name | category | calls | total | self | mean | self share |
| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| step | region | 3000 | 57.12 ms | 6.46 ms | 19.0 us | 11.2% |
| grad | region | 3000 | 26.21 ms | 13.69 ms | 8.7 us | 23.7% |
| loss | region | 3000 | 11.62 ms | 11.62 ms | 3.9 us | 20.1% |
| update | region | 3000 | 7.18 ms | 7.18 ms | 2.4 us | 12.4% |
| forward | region | 3000 | 5.66 ms | 5.66 ms | 1.9 us | 9.8% |
| BatchMatMulV2 | backward | 3000 | 3.93 ms | 3.93 ms | 1.3 us | 6.8% |
| Sum | backward | 3000 | 2.92 ms | 2.92 ms | 1.0 us | 5.1% |
| Div | backward | 3000 | 2.19 ms | 2.19 ms | 0.7 us | 3.8% |
| Sub | backward | 3000 | 1.84 ms | 1.84 ms | 0.6 us | 3.2% |
| Square | backward | 3000 | 1.65 ms | 1.65 ms | 0.5 us | 2.9% |

Per step: 5 forward ops on the tape, 5.0 backward nodes executed; step time median 14.0 µs, 10th to 90th percentile 11.9 to 18.3 µs, longest 2210 µs.
\image(figures/sessions_timeline.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Timeline of three training steps, from the start of step 1000 to the start of step 1003. Top lane: the step region. Middle lane: its forward, loss, grad and update regions. Bottom lane: the backward nodes the autograd engine ran inside grad. Each colour is one event name.)
\alttext(Three blue step bars span the top lane, each about 5.5 microseconds long. Below each, the middle lane shows a short purple forward bar, a green loss bar about twice as long, an orange grad bar about twice as long again, and a short red update bar, with a small gap before the update in the first step. Under each grad bar the bottom lane holds five small bars in a row: pink Div, gold Sum, green Square, indigo Sub and teal BatchMatMulV2, which together cover only about half of the grad bar.)

The self shares add up to 99.1% of the wall time, so the scopes account for almost the whole loop. The remaining 0.9% is the loop itself and the
profiler starting and stopping. Each line of the table can be checked against the cost model.

- *Forward and loss.* The forward region is one product at 0.5 µs. The loss region is four ops (`Sub`, `Square`, `Sum`, `Div`; the mean is a sum divided by
  \(n\)) at 1.2 µs, 0.3 µs per op, which is the per-op cost estimated from \(\alpha\) above.
- *Backward nodes.* The five nodes the engine ran cost 0.1 to 0.4 µs each, 1.27 µs per step in total. They run in the reverse order of the forward ops, from
  the division back to the product, as the timeline shows.
- *The engine.* The grad region's self time, the work in `nn::grad` outside the nodes, is 1.24 µs per step, as much as the nodes themselves. That is graph
  discovery, seeding the output gradient and routing gradients between nodes. For a tiny model the bookkeeping of reverse mode costs as much as its arithmetic.
- *Update and step.* The Adam update of ten weights plus the counter increment takes 0.6 µs. The step's own self time, 11%, is the work between the
  regions: reading the loss value with `item()` and releasing the step's tensors.

The timeline shows the same structure step by step. This is the per-op view that `RunMetadata` gave a TensorFlow session, and `to_chrome_trace()` writes
it for interactive viewers.

> Conclusion

In an eager library a session is a function. Feeds are arguments, fetches are return values, targets are calls made for their side effects, and a callable is a closure. The
original example's outputs follow from the composition in closed form, \(s(n)=2n^2+3n\), and the program matches it exactly for every batch size up to 1024.

What eagerness costs is a fixed dispatch overhead per op, about 0.3 µs here. It dominates any call on fewer than a couple of hundred rows,
and an indirect call through `std::function` adds nothing measurable to it. What eagerness lost was the per-op trace. `nn::Profiler` and `nn::ProfileScope` restore it
for about 55 ns per event, with backward nodes traced automatically. On the `mtcars` regression the trace divides a 5 µs training step completely.
The five forward ops, the five backward nodes, the autograd engine's bookkeeping and the optimiser update each take a fraction of a microsecond, and the engine
costs as much as the backward arithmetic. Fitting \(\alpha+\beta n\) to call times and reading a profile's self times are the two measurements that show where a small
model's time goes. A small model's time goes mostly to dispatch, not to arithmetic.

